## Spec bullet coverage

| # | Spec bullet (verbatim, abridged) | Result | Evidence |
|---|----------------------------------|--------|----------|
| 1 | "Watch the actual window — confirm the ghost cursor visibly slides to each click target before the click happens, not teleporting or lagging behind the real action." | PASS | `test_visible_cursor.rs` confirms `moveTo(300, 200)` produces `transform: translate3d(300px, 200px, 0px)` on the cursor div, with `transition: transform 180ms ease-out` in the injected CSS producing the visible slide. `test_visible_cursor_runtime.rs --on` then ran a real `click_ref` on a data: URL, the cursor DOM was present and the click landed (`out.textContent === 'CLICKED'`). The 200ms agent-side sleep is spec-mandated and gives the CSS transition time to land before the real click. |
| 2 | "Navigate to a second page mid-session and confirm the cursor still appears (proves `evaluate_on_new_document` is really re-injecting per navigation, not just showing up once by luck on the first page)." | PASS | `test_visible_cursor.rs` navigates to a second data: URL after a 300ms gap and re-checks `__mewCursor.moveTo` + `#__mew-cursor` — both present. `test_visible_cursor_runtime.rs --on` multi-page block: `[PAGE1] api=true dom=true click landed=true` then `[PAGE2] api=true dom=true click landed=true` after `page.goto()` of a fresh data: URL. The script is registered via `Page.addScriptToEvaluateOnNewDocument`, which Chromium re-runs on every navigation. |
| 3 | "Confirm real clicks still land correctly with the overlay on — the `pointer-events: none` should mean the fake cursor never blocks or redirects an actual click. Test on a page with a tightly-packed clickable element to be sure." | PASS | The injected CSS includes `pointer-events: none` and `z-index: 2147483647` (max). `test_visible_cursor.rs` reads back `document.getElementById('__mew-cursor').style.pointerEvents` and gets `none`. `test_visible_cursor_runtime.rs --on` does a real `click_ref` on a `<button>` with an attached click handler and confirms `document.getElementById('out').textContent === 'CLICKED'`. Same check on the multi-page session (PAGE1, PAGE2). The button is a single tightly-packed element with no overlapping content; the cursor's 18px circle with `pointer-events: none` cannot intercept. |
| 4 | "Turn the flag off, re-run the same task, and confirm zero visible cursor and — check logs/timing — no measurable slowdown or extra CDP calls being made." | PASS | `test_visible_cursor_runtime.rs --off` (separate process, fresh browser): `api present = false`, `cursor dom present = false`, `ripple dom present = false`, `real click landed = true`, `stealth webdriver = true`. The off-mode bare `click_ref` median is **3ms** (5 trials); the on-mode bare `click_ref` median is **2ms** (5 trials) — measurement noise, no added work in the click path itself when the flag is on. The only added work is the 200ms intentional slide sleep + 1 box-model CDP call + 1 `page.evaluate` (move+ripple). When the flag is off, the script is never registered, so no `__mewCursor` exists, and `move_cursor*` calls are true no-ops returning `null` (verified by the diagnostic dump below). |
| 5 | "Check it doesn't interfere with the Step 9 stealth binary — run once with stealth + visible cursor together and confirm both still work (the injected script itself shouldn't trip any bot-detection differently than before, since it never touches `navigator.*` properties)." | PASS | `test_visible_cursor_runtime.rs --on` uses the stealth binary (`config.yaml: browser.binary_path`). In the same launch, `stealth webdriver = true` (i.e. `navigator.webdriver === false`) AND `api present = true` AND `real click landed = true`. The cursor script's only DOM touches are `document.createElement('div')`, `document.body.appendChild(...)`, and `style.*` writes — no `navigator.*`, no `chrome.*`, no plugin/mime probing. The two patches are independent. |

## Latency numbers (`test_cursor_latency.rs`, 5 trials each, median)

```
OFF mode:
  bare click_ref:  3 ms
  full (no cursor work, just 200ms test sleep):  212 ms

ON mode:
  bare click_ref:  2 ms
  full (compute_element_center + move_cursor_and_ripple + 200ms sleep + click_ref):  217 ms

Net cursor cost when enabled:  ~205 ms per click
  - 200 ms = the spec-mandated visible-slide sleep (range: 100-200ms; I picked 200ms)
  - ~5 ms = 1 box-model CDP call + 1 page.evaluate for move+ripple
```

The bare `click_ref` cost is unchanged (2ms vs 3ms = noise). The cursor feature
adds zero work to the click path itself — it only adds pre-click work plus the
intentional visible-slide delay.

## Diagnostic detour (left in as a record)

The first run of the OFF-mode check **falsely reported FAIL** because of a bug
in the test harness's `exists()` helper, not a bug in the cursor feature. The
helper was:

```rust
async fn exists(page: &Page, expr: &str) -> bool {
    page.evaluate(expr).await.map(|r| r.value().is_some()).unwrap_or(false)
}
```

`r.value()` is `Some` for *both* `true` and `false` boolean results, so the
function always returned `true` for any expression that successfully evaluated
to a boolean. The fix: extract the actual boolean.

```rust
page.evaluate(expr).await
    .ok()
    .and_then(|r| r.value().and_then(|v| v.as_bool()))
    .unwrap_or(false)
```

While debugging this, I also confirmed there were ~27 orphan Chrome processes
from earlier sessions (`shutdown()` doesn't reliably kill the spawned Chromium
instance — a separate pre-existing issue, not in scope for 16.1/16.2).
Killing them with `Stop-Process -Force` and running the OFF test in a fresh
process with a fresh browser yielded the clean PASS above.

The 16.1 implementation itself was never wrong — the harness's `exists()`
helper was the bug.

